264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337739" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337739" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337739" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "linear"] ["text" "nonlinear"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337739" "field" "field-6" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "nonlinear"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=xy\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $xy,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-337777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=1+{x}^{2},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-338104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\,y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337774" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=(x-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=x-1$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=2x.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=4,{f}_{2}(x)=0,{f}_{1}(x)=-{x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337765" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x},{f}_{1}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-2.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=2,{f}_{2}(x)={e}^{x},{f}_{1}(x)=0,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337778" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-337778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-337778" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})={sin}^{2}⁡x+4$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})+2L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+2\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+4) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x+8 \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)+8 \\\\  & =2+8 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-64900" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}-3x$ and $L({y}_{2})=x(x+1)$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}+3{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}+3{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})+3L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3\\cdot x(x+1) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-3x+3{x}^{2}+3x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3{x}^{2})+(3x-3x) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-130076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{x}^{2}"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}+6x"] ["math" "{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+3"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{x}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2-{sin}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{cos}^{2}⁡x$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2-{sin}^{2}⁡x)-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3{sin}^{2}⁡x-3{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =6-3({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =6-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-130080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130080" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=3x-5$ and $L({y}_{2})=(x-3)^{2}$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3x-5)+(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6x-10+{x}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(6x-6x)+(-10+9) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-130081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+12x-1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+6x-1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-1"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-130081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{2}-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-65036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}+4$ and $L({y}_{2})=3(x-2)^{2}$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-65036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot ({x}^{2}+4)-3(x-2)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+12-3({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+12-3{x}^{2}+12x-12 \\\\  & =12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-65036" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "daa03b1e971a9bd14d6e1de601a82dccaeff80b6a805a0c9d410d350f02ff85d"} {"question" "q-65036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-65036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12x"] ["math" "-24"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "12x"] ["math" "6{x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "q-65036" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/state.json" "file_sha256" "f0e97ba76e38bbb20dfcdf789f98364f30ac6ec71e5218c26a122e4264b7307d"} {"question" "e-6572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Show that the differential operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nis nonlinear." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/example-6572.json" "file_sha256" "e2845bd713229cabf58c94e470f94622fe20a09fd120b89e9b1add26666030aa"} {"question" "e-6572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & +a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & +ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(ay_1+by_2)  \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2),$ the operator $D$ is nonlinear." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/example-6572.json" "file_sha256" "e2845bd713229cabf58c94e470f94622fe20a09fd120b89e9b1add26666030aa"} {"question" "e-21786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n\n- $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/example-21786.json" "file_sha256" "299b21b964722794ec115c6619b884889e8a9b2c5288082a0200de3c7077c38a"} {"question" "e-21786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator $D_1$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x),$ and $f_0(x) = 3.$\n\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0,$ and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/example-21786.json" "file_sha256" "299b21b964722794ec115c6619b884889e8a9b2c5288082a0200de3c7077c38a"} {"question" "e-6569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/example-6569.json" "file_sha256" "7c08e6aa710299e366c8e81d4f62701c266a8980a1519b8a413261eb81032a1a"} {"question" "e-6569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity $1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x,$ we can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037933/example-6569.json" "file_sha256" "7c08e6aa710299e366c8e81d4f62701c266a8980a1519b8a413261eb81032a1a"}] "usage" {"e-6572" [] "e-6569" [] "e-21786" []} "basis" 1952 "retractions" [[17592186228901 :question/problem "Show that the differential operator\n\n$D(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$\n\nis nonlinear."] [17592186228901 :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L$, we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x)$, and where $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(ay_{1} + by_{2}) &= (ay_{1} + by_{2})\\frac{d^{2}}{dx^{2}}(ay_{1} + by_{2}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(ay_{1} + by_{2}) \\\\\n&= (ay_{1} + by_{2})(a\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}}) + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= + a^{2}y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + aby_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + aby_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b^{2}y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} \\\\\n&= + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= a^{2}y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + b^{2}y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx} \\\\\n&= + ab(y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\naD(y_{1}) + bD(y_{2}) &= a[y_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + \\frac{dy_{1}}{dx}] + b[y_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + \\frac{dy_{2}}{dx}] \\\\\n&= ay_{1}\\frac{d^{2}y_{1}}{dx^{2}} + by_{2}\\frac{d^{2}y_{2}}{dx^{2}} + a\\frac{dy_{1}}{dx} + b\\frac{dy_{2}}{dx}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\nD(ay_1+by_2) \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2)\n$$\n\nthe operator $D$ is nonlinear."] [17592186228885 :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n1. $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n2. $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n3. $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$"] [17592186228885 :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator $D_1$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y)$, which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n$D_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y$\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x)$, and $f_0(x) = 3$.\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n$D_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y$\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0$, and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186228900 :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)$?\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta$.*"] [17592186228900 :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L$, we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(2y_{1} - y_{2}) &= 2L(y_{1}) - L(y_{2}) \\\\\n&= 2\\tan^{2} x - 2\\sec^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity\n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nwe can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(2y_{1} - y_{2}) &= 2\\tan^{2} x - 2(1 + \\tan^{2} x) \\\\\n&=-2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
